From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 21:40:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10194/11362] Close two evidence holes in the REQ-HARNESS-5920
 node-id delta

Both were demonstrated by execution before anything was written, and both are
the same shape the module's own commit message warned about: an unmeasured
failure reading as a clean delta.

1. An ABORTED suite passed as ready. The 2026-08-27 collection abort prints
   zero FAILED lines, so the stdout path parsed an empty failure set and
   delta() answered ready_allowed=True with a delta of -1,726 -- a suite that
   never ran read as cleaner than baseline. Verified live before the fix.

2. A new setup ERROR was invisible. The parser read only FAILED lines; a
   change that turns a passing test into a setup error introduces no FAILED
   node id and passed the delta. This is the exact suppression that
   contaminated two full-suite runs (failures became setup errors and the
   count DROPPED).

The fix adds one evidence gate, observed_suite_evidence(stdout, exit_code):
None when the run aborted (Interrupted / error during collection /
INTERNALERROR), and None when a nonzero exit is explained by no parsed node.
None fails closed in exp6682's caller, which now routes its stdout path
through the gate.

ERROR nodes get their own parser and their own ledger, deliberately separate:
the committed baseline records 1,726 FAILED nodes and NOT the 143 ERROR nodes
its run also saw, so enforcing errors against an empty set would refuse every
honest run -- a check that cries wolf trains people to bypass it. Enforcement
activates only when the baseline records baseline_error_node_ids AND the
caller measured this run's; until then delta() reports
error_nodes_assessed=false out loud instead of claiming clean. The next
re-baseline should record the error ledger.

Mutation-proved: deleting the abort rule, the unexplained-exit rule, the
new-error veto, or the ERROR parser each turns the new suite RED (1-2 tests),
and reverting exp6682's caller to the naive parse turns its regression test
RED. Module restored byte-identical after each proof. Focused suites: 14/14
and 16/16.

The module had NO dedicated tests when it shipped; tests/python/
test_global_suite_baseline.py now pins every rule, including the fail-toward-
not-ready direction on an unreadable baseline and the count/list consistency
of the committed ledger.

Two test-suite mutation markers were deleted deliberately rather than
resolved by checkout: both flagged only [NOT this run] paths
(python/carnot/agentic/arc_induction_tools.py and arc_induction_tool_loop.py)
-- a concurrent agent's in-flight authored work observed during this session's
pytest window, not test damage. Reverting them is the exact destroy-authored-
work failure the marker text itself warns about.

